    "title": "Hypothetical VRAM Planning Checklist for 12GB GPUs (LocalLLaMA)",
    "body": "This guide outlines a hypothetical VRAM planning checklist for users with a 12 GB GPU running local language models. It's crucial to remember this is a theoretical exercise, not based on actual tested models or runtimes. We'll break down VRAM usage into three key areas: model weights, the KV cache, and other runtime allocations.  Understanding these components is vital for efficient local LLM deployment.\n\nModel weights represent the largest portion of VRAM. The size of the model directly dictates how much space it occupies. The KV cache, used during inference for attention mechanisms, grows dynamically with context length. Finally, other runtime allocations include the operating system, the LLM framework itself, and any auxiliary processes.  This checklist aims to provide a structured approach to estimating VRAM needs in a hypothetical scenario, helping you gauge potential model compatibility and performance before committing to a specific setup. No actual models or runtimes were tested in developing this guide.",
