    "title": "A Theoretical VRAM Allocation Framework for 12GB Local LLM Setups",
    "body": "This document presents a hypothetical framework for VRAM planning on a 12 GB GPU for local language models. All scenarios discussed are purely theoretical and have not been validated through actual testing. The objective is to provide a structured method for anticipating resource needs. We will delineate VRAM usage into three distinct categories: static model weight storage, dynamic KV cache requirements, and miscellaneous runtime overhead.  This separation helps in understanding where VRAM is consumed during LLM operation.\n\nModel weights are the foundational data, consuming a fixed amount of VRAM based on the model's architecture and precision. The KV cache, essential for efficient text generation, scales with the input sequence length and batch size. Other runtime allocations encompass the Python interpreter, libraries like PyTorch or TensorFlow, CUDA kernels, and system processes. This theoretical checklist serves as a preliminary guide to help assess feasibility for different models on a 12GB card, acknowledging that no specific models or runtimes were benchmarked.",
